questions.answer:
function delegate(root, selector, type, handler) {
  root.addEventListener(type, (event) => {
    const matched = event.target.closest(selector);
    if (!matched || !root.contains(matched)) return;
    handler(event, matched);
  });
}

const list = document.getElementById("team");
delegate(list, "li", "click", (event, item) => {
  console.log("selected", item.dataset.id);
});

closest walks up from the real target to the nearest matching element. The root.contains check prevents a match outside the delegated root when the selector is generic — without it, a click on a nested list's li could be handled by the wrong root. The guard returns early for clicks on padding. The listener is attached once, so rows added later work automatically. To support capture or options, accept an options argument and pass it through.

