questions.answer:
function onOutsideClick(element, handler) {
  const controller = new AbortController();

  document.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
    if (!element.contains(event.target)) handler(event);
  }, { signal: controller.signal });

  return () => controller.abort();
}

const menu = document.getElementById("menu");
const stop = onOutsideClick(menu, () => console.log("closed"));
// later: stop();

The listener lives on document so it sees every click, including clicks on elements added later. element.contains(event.target) checks whether the click started inside the menu; if not, the handler runs. The AbortController ties the listener's lifetime to the returned cleanup function, so calling stop() removes it without keeping a handler reference. A click on the menu that is stopped with stopPropagation will not reach document, which may be desirable or a bug depending on the design.

